3. Security Reflection & User Provisioning
Security Risks of Using root for Backend Connections
Over-Privileged Access: The root user possesses unrestricted administrative privileges (e.g., DROP DATABASE, modifying system variables, altering user permissions). If an application backend connected via root suffers a SQL Injection vulnerability, an attacker can gain full control over the database server, modify sensitive data across schemas, or execute host-level system commands.
Lack of Isolation: Multiple applications using the root user share identical access rights. A compromise in one application exposes all other databases hosted on the same server.
Auditability & Compliance: Operating with a single superuser account obscures activity logging, making it difficult to trace security incidents or comply with the Principle of Least Privilege.
Least-Privileged User Provisioning Commands
SQL
 1. Create a dedicated application user bound to localhost
CREATE USER 'app_user'@'localhost' IDENTIFIED BY 'SecurePassword123!';

2. Grant minimum required privileges restricted to the 'school' database
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON school.* TO 'app_user'@'localhost';

3. Apply privilege changes
FLUSH PRIVILEGES;

